## 3. Relatedness check screen **[NEW]**

**What it shows:** Appears after upload when 2+ files are loaded, before the dashboard. One row per pair (or per cluster, for larger N) of runs, each with a confidence badge and the plain-language reasoning behind it.

**What it means:** This is the automated version of the manual check done by hand in the earlier analysis — reading each run's actual prompt text and starting git state to judge whether they're really the same task before trusting any comparison built on top of them. It's advisory, not a hard gate: the user can always proceed to view logs individually even if they're flagged unrelated (see open question in the spec doc — this default can change).

| # | Component | Detail |
|---|---|---|
| 3.1 | Heading | "Checking task relatedness across 4 runs" (count is dynamic) |
| 3.2 | Per-pair/cluster row | Run names + a confidence badge: **Related** (green dot), **Review recommended** (yellow dot), **Unrelated** (red dot) |
| 3.3 | Reasoning line under each row | Plain text, e.g. *"Both prompts describe implementing a monthly-budget feature on the same repository (same branch, same base commit)."* or *"Prompt A describes a budgeting feature; Prompt C describes an authentication refactor — these do not appear to be the same task."* |
| 3.4 | Partial-confidence case | *"Same repository, but Run B started from a different commit than Runs A/C/D — review before trusting this comparison."* (yellow) |
| 3.5 | Primary action | **"Continue to comparison"** — enabled regardless of result |
| 3.6 | Secondary action (only shown if any row is Unrelated or Review) | **"View runs individually instead"** — skips the head-to-head dashboard and opens N single-run views |

