## 9. Top driver panel **[UPDATED — was a hardcoded "Root cause" narrative, now generated]**

**What it shows:** Same visual treatment as v1's "Root cause" card (label, heading, paragraph, mini-stats row) — but the content is no longer specific to the browser-automation finding from one particular comparison. It's generated per-comparison from whichever single factor contributes most to the largest KPI drift.

**What it means:** v1's version was correct for that specific pair of logs but hardcoded — a general product needs this panel to identify *whatever* the dominant driver actually is for the runs loaded, which could be a different mechanism entirely (e.g., a longer prompt, a different tool policy, more retries) depending on the data.

| # | Element | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| 9.1 | Heading | Auto-generated one-line summary of the top driver (e.g., "A browser-automation excursion, not prompt wording" was correct for that comparison; for a different comparison it might read "A retry loop on one run's test suite, not prompt wording") |
| 9.2 | Body paragraph | Auto-generated explanation citing the specific tool calls / metrics behind the claim, same style/specificity as v1's paragraph |
| 9.3 | Mini-stats row | 2–3 supporting numbers, chosen based on which raw metrics the narrative cites (not a fixed three slots) |
| 9.4 | **[NEW] Multi-driver case** | If no single factor clearly dominates (e.g., drift is spread evenly across several unrelated metrics), the panel says so explicitly — *"No single dominant driver identified; see the full comparison table for the complete picture"* — rather than forcing a narrative where one doesn't exist |

